  "final_text": "**No, not as it is.** The two files show four problems:\n\n1. **The certificate has expired.** It ran until 31.08.2026. The renewal inspection is booked for 22.10.2026, so as of 16 October you have no valid certificate. (My system clock says 26 October. If that's right, the inspection may already have happened, but these files don't include a new certificate.) Until a renewed certificate is issued, don't make any \"certified organic\" claim.\n\n2. **Only 12 of your 40 ha are certified (30%).** \"Certified organic vineyards\" in the plural, with no qualifier, suggests all your vineyards are certified. The other 28 ha are not, so the claim is misleading as written.\n\n3. **It would sit next to all your wines.** Most of your roughly 550,000 bottles likely come at least partly from uncertified land. Putting the claim beside every wine links it to products it doesn't cover.\n\n4. **The cellar and winemaking are not certified.** Under the EU organic regulation (2018/848), organic terms on wine labels and in sales material are generally for products made by a certified operator. Wine from a non-certified cellar can't be presented as organic, and a vineyard claim beside the bottle can read that way. This matters because 74% of your sales go to Germany, Sweden and Poland. The EU also tightened its rules on green claims (Directive 2024/825, applying from 27 September 2026). Those rules target claims that are really about only part of a business or product.\n\n**What you can do:**\n- Until the renewal comes through, use no organic wording on shelf cards.\n- After renewal, a narrow, factual statement about the company is safer. For example: \"12 of our 40 ha of vineyards are certified organic (control body Biosertifika Kontrola, XX-BIO-000)\". Keep it off the cards next to individual wines unless those wines qualify.\n- If you want organic claims on particular wines, get the cellar and winemaking certified too. Then you could make the claim for wines made only from the 12 certified hectares.\n- Before printing anything, get the exact wording approved by Biosertifika Kontrola, and by your importers for the German, Swedish and Polish markets. I can't confirm how the North Macedonian rules or each export market's enforcement apply in your case.",
